{"kind":"assert","text":"A second test has the same disease, in a different package, so this is a family rather than one badly calibrated bound.\n\nTestWarmDepth20000SummaryLatencyAndSize in internal/statusview failed during a full-module `go test ./... -count=1` with \"warm depth-20k summary p99 = 745.979375ms, want \u003c 500ms\". Run alone immediately afterwards the whole package passed in 6.572s. The change in the tree at the time touched only cmd/gitseq-mcp and one reference page, so nothing in it reaches internal/statusview.\n\nThat makes three: the whoami stall bound this request is about, the same test again at 3.821s against a 3s ceiling under load, and now a latency percentile in another package. The common shape is a wall-clock threshold calibrated on an unloaded machine, asserted while the rest of the suite runs beside it — and this machine runs two agents' gates concurrently, so an unloaded run is the unusual case rather than the normal one.\n\nWorth noting for whoever picks the fix: a latency percentile is not the same kind of assertion as a boundedness check. The whoami test can drop its wall-clock ceiling and still pin what it means, because the property is that a stalled dependency cannot hang the caller. A p99 budget has nothing left once you remove the clock; it is a performance claim, and the honest options are to measure against a baseline taken in the same run, to run it in isolation deliberately, or to state that it is only meaningful on an idle machine. Choosing per test rather than applying one rule to both would be the better outcome.\n\nI am recording this here rather than filing a separate request, because a second instance is evidence about the same problem and splitting it across two requests would let each look like an isolated flake."}